<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/expert/investigative-methods">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the capstone of the journalism ladder, and it assumes the craft you have already
    practised. <em>The Inverted Pyramid</em> and <em>Interviewing</em> gave you the daily
    reporting rhythms this lesson deliberately inverts; <em>News vs Opinion vs Analysis</em>
    drew the evidentiary lines an investigation must hold under legal attack; and the advanced
    pair <em>Data Journalism</em> and <em>Media Law and Ethics</em> supplied the document
    pipeline and the legal limits this page runs at maximum stakes. Every method lesson above
    hardens here into a case that must survive lawyers, editors and the subject's own counsel.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>construct story ideas from paper gaps — comparing what should be against what is — and apply the three-part assessment test before committing months;</li>
    <li>build a master file that turns reporting into a case, with every load-bearing sentence carrying its evidence;</li>
    <li>plan the three modes of the evidence interview, and sequence the accountability interview correctly;</li>
    <li>run the bulletproofing pass: adversarial fact-checking, document forensics, recomputed numbers and the written response file;</li>
    <li>handle leaked data, source protection and failure honestly — the disciplines that keep the method credible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Investigative journalism is the profession's <strong>long-form verification discipline</strong>
    — months of documents, sources and cross-checking aimed at what powerful people prefer
    unreported. It is this ladder's capstone: every method lesson above, at the highest stakes
    the craft knows.
  </p>
  <p>
    What separates investigation from daily reporting is not intensity but <em>structure</em>.
    A news story verifies a claim; an investigation assembles a case — a hypothesis about
    wrongdoing, tested against records, witnesses and numbers, with the strongest possible
    version of the counter-case written by the subject themselves before publication. The
    unit's classic craft stages (finding a story, reporting it, interviewing, writing it,
    editing it) do not disappear at this level; they harden into a pipeline where each stage
    has a legal and evidentiary threshold. Watergate (1972–74) remains the template: a local
    burglary followed through money trails (the checking of a cashier's cheque routed through
    a Nixon re-election committee slush fund) into a presidency — two reporters, one court
    source called Deep Throat, and an editor who demanded a second source for every
    paragraph that could end a career.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="documents, sources, bulletproofing, impact" widget="equationStep" config={invCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps run in dependency order. Step 1 puts
    documents at the spine of the case — the misconception that investigations live on secret
    sources falls in the first section below. Step 2 adds the human channel and its protection
    duties, step 3 the verification standard that makes the case bulletproof, and step 4 the
    impact duties that outlast publication. Reveal one step at a time and match each against
    the section that expands it — the panel is the pipeline, compressed.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> investigation is not intensified news work but a different
  structure — a hypothesis about wrongdoing, tested against records, witnesses and numbers,
  with the counter-case printed before publication. The first craft stage is finding a story
  worth months of that structure.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="slide between the daily story and the assembled case" widget="compare" config={invStructureCompare} />

  <h2>Finding the story: what makes news worth the months</h2>
  <p>
    Investigative story ideas rarely arrive as tips; they are <em>constructed from
    anomalies</em>. The method: compare what should be against what is. A city that
    demolished 40 buildings in a year where its peers demolished four; a judge whose
    docket runs twice the norm; a charity whose programme spending falls from 90 cents on
    the dollar to 30 after a new director arrives. Each is a paper gap, and public records
    make them findable: property registries, court filings, procurement awards, campaign
    finance, inspection reports, budgets. The assessment test before committing months is
    ruthless — <em>so what?</em> (does someone with power or money gain?), <em>prove
    what?</em> (can the claim be evidenced to publication standard, or is it a suspicion?),
    and <em>who could sue?</em> (libel exposure is priced before the first interview).
    The classic false start is the story that "feels wrong" — feelings generate leads;
    they cannot survive libel proceedings.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that investigative reporters rely mainly on secret
  sources and hidden cameras. The spine of nearly every landmark investigation is
  <em>public documents</em> — filings any citizen could request — with sources adding
  explanation and direction. If your story lives only in what a whistleblower says and
  dies without the records, it is not ready.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet first — the spine of a landmark investigation" widget="predict" config={invSpinePredict} />
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> a story worth months must pass the ruthless test — someone with
  power or money gains, the claim can be evidenced to publication standard, and the libel
  exposure is priced. With a paper gap in hand, the reporting rhythm inverts: build the case
  first, publish last.</p>

  <h2>Reporting and writing: the case, not the story</h2>
  <p>
    Investigative reporting inverts the daily news rhythm. Instead of publishing fast and
    updating, the reporter builds a <em>master file</em> — every document chronologised,
    every fact sourced to a document or a named (or protected) source, every unresolved
    contradiction flagged in writing — so that when lawyers, editors and the subject's
    own counsel attack, the case holds. Writing follows the same discipline: the narrative
    leads with the strongest documented scene, but every load-bearing sentence carries its
    evidence; the feature-writing toolkit (character, scene, tension) sells the reading,
    while the sourcing apparatus (numbered documents, quoted records) carries the weight.
    The two-source rule is the floor, not the ceiling: material allegations get document
    corroboration where documents exist, and the reporters' notebook records the exact
    question that produced each quote — misquoted subjects are the cheapest way to lose a
    lawsuit and a story.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — the sourcing floor" widget="cloze" config={invSourceCloze} />

  <h2>The interview as evidence session</h2>
  <p>
    At this level the interview is a formal evidentiary instrument with three modes.
    The <em>accountability interview</em> — confronting the subject with the documented
    findings — comes last, after the case is assembled, and its craft is precision:
    open with the least confrontational factual question, present documents one at a
    time, and let the silence after each do its work; the transcript becomes the
    subject's own words on the record. The <em>insider interview</em> (the retired
    official, the former employee) trades on specificity — people talk when asked
    about their documents, not about their morals. The <em>cooperating-source
    interview</em> with a whistleblower adds protection logistics: secure channels,
    assumptions about metadata (a document handed over with intact properties can
    identify its leaker), and agreements about what each side may publish. Skilled
    interviewers plan all three before booking a room, because a subject warned early
    can destroy records, coordinate alibis or pre-empt with legal action.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each evidence-interview mode with its logic" widget="match" config={invInterviewMatch} />
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the case is assembled in a master file and the interview has
  become an evidence session in three modes, with the confrontation deliberately staged last.
  What remains is institutional: the editors, the lawyers and the decision to publish.</p>

  <h2>Sub-editing, editorial decision and the law</h2>
  <p>
    The final craft stage is institutional. Sub-editing an investigation means checking
    every factual sentence against the master file — a full read of the evidence, not a
    style pass — and the editorial decision to publish weighs four questions in order:
    Is it true to the evidence? Is it in the public interest (corruption, danger to the
    public — not private embarrassment)? Is it fair (the subject's full response sought
    and printed, the strongest counter-case represented)? Can we prove it in court
    (libel law's burden, source-protection law's limits)? Media lawyers read the draft
    sentence by sentence; the phrase "reports suggest" is never allowed to carry a
    factual allegation — the allegation is either sourced or cut. This is why
    investigations retract so rarely: the process is designed so that a failed story
    dies in the editing room rather than on the record.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild the four editorial questions, in order" widget="order" config={invEditorialOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the subject's denial is an obstacle the
  investigation must survive. The right-of-reply is part of the evidence — a subject
  who misstates a fact the documents contradict has just confirmed the documents, and a
  subject who declines to respond is reported as declining. Fairness is not a courtesy;
  it is both legal armour and the method's own verification layer.</p>

  <h2>The collaborative era and impact</h2>
  <p>
    The lone-scoop myth dissolved in the offshore-leaks era. The ICIJ's model — the
    Panama Papers (2016): 11.5 million leaked documents from one law firm, processed by
    roughly 370 journalists across some 80 countries, published simultaneously — turned
    jurisdictional exposure into safety in numbers and made document <em>processing</em>
    (search, entity-matching, visualization — the data-journalism lesson's pipeline) as
    central as source work. Impact, meanwhile, is a duty rather than a climax:
    investigations are followed up, corrections owned when the case misses, and sources
    protected for years after publication — the reporter's relationship with the person
    who risked a career does not end at the printer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the magnitude — the Panama Papers cache" widget="estimate" config={invPanamaEstimate} />

  <h2>Verification: the bulletproofing pass</h2>
  <p>
    Before lawyers, before editing, the investigation runs its own adversarial
    <em>fact-check</em> — an editor or researcher who did not report the story attacks the
    draft as if hired by the subject. Every factual sentence is traced to its evidence
    (document, on-record interview, dataset); every name, title, date and figure is
    re-verified against the master file; every quote is checked against the recording, not
    the notebook. Documents get their own forensics: metadata (creation dates, authorship
    fields), cross-referencing against independent registries (does the company in the
    filing exist in the companies register with the same directors?), and consistency
    checks across the leak (the same bank account appearing in unrelated records is
    corroboration; a mismatch is a question). Numbers get recomputed from raw data —
    the data-journalism lesson's pipeline is also a verification instrument, because a
    rate recomputed from source tables often disagrees with the press release that
    started the story. The pass ends with a written <em>response file</em>: the subject's
    answers logged against each allegation, so the published piece can say precisely
    what was asked, answered and denied.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty line — the bulletproofing pass" widget="spot-error" config={invBulletproofSpot} />

  <h2>Working with leaked and stolen data</h2>
  <p>
    The offshore-leaks era made a new craft question routine: <em>may</em> journalism
    publish what a thief or a hacker stole? The working consensus, argued from the
    Pentagon Papers tradition, is that newsrooms may report on leaked material in the
    public interest without endorsing how it was obtained — but the ethics are applied,
    not assumed: reporters assess whether the leak looks like whistleblowing or
    disinformation, verify a sample of documents against independent records before
    trusting the rest, and weigh the harms of publication (lives, ongoing investigations,
    private individuals swept into public datasets) against the accountability gain.
    Operationally, leaked-data work is data engineering under secrecy: air-gapped or
    hardened analysis environments, link analysis across millions of records (the
    Panama Papers' 11.5 million documents were made navigable by entity-matching —
    every company, officer and intermediary resolved into a graph), and publication
    discipline that publishes the story, never the source's identity. The 2016
    offshore-leak collaboration's editors called the method "radical sharing" — every
    partner newsroom sees everything — and its result inverted the scoop economy: the
    story was bigger than any single outlet could have made it, and no one outlet
    could be silenced into killing it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the leaked-data publication decision" widget="branch" config={invLeakBranch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that investigation is about catching bad individuals.
  The landmark investigations indict <em>systems</em>: Spotlight's finding was not one
  predator priest but an institutional rotation policy that reassigned offenders for
  decades; Watergate was not a burglary crew but a culture of political espionage with
  its financing apparatus. The individual is the entry point; the pattern, the incentive
  and the machinery that protected the individual are the story — and the reform that
  follows.</p>

  <p>
    Source protection, at this level, is an operational discipline rather than a
    promise. The craft: encrypted channels agreed in advance (with an understanding
    of what each tool does and does not hide — the cryptography lesson's distinctions
    apply to human sources too), compartmentalisation (the reporter who knows the
    source's identity keeps it out of notes, drafts and calendars), metadata hygiene
    (documents re-typed or scrubbed before publication so embedded properties cannot
    identify the leaker), and honesty about the limits — a newsroom can protect a
    name from its rivals far better than it can protect it from a court subpoena in
    a jurisdiction without shield laws. The correspondent's rule of thumb: never
    promise a source what you cannot technically guarantee, and never let their
    trust substitute for your verification.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline's honesty also covers failure. Investigations sometimes miss —
    a document misread, a source with an agenda, a pattern that dissolves under the
    subject's evidence — and the profession's credibility is built by how that is
    handled: corrections at full prominence, the method shown so readers can see
    where the reasoning broke, and the subject's vindication reported with the same
    energy as the allegation. An investigative desk that never publishes a
    correction is not a perfect desk; it is an unaudited one.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three modes of the evidence interview, and say where in the case timeline the accountability interview belongs — and why.</li>
    <li>A charity's programme spending falls from 90 cents on the dollar to 30 after a new director arrives. Walk the claim through the three-part assessment test before committing months to it.</li>
    <li>Why is the subject's right-of-reply part of the evidence rather than a courtesy?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Accountability (confronting the subject with documented
    findings), insider (trading on specificity — people talk about their documents, not their
    morals) and cooperating-source (whistleblower, with protection logistics). The
    accountability interview comes last, after the case is assembled, because a subject warned
    early can destroy records, coordinate alibis or pre-empt with legal action. (2) <em>So
    what?</em> — someone with power or money gains if the ratio shift is real. <em>Prove
    what?</em> — the claim must be evidenced to publication standard from the charity's own
    filings and accounts, not from suspicion. <em>Who could sue?</em> — libel exposure is
    priced against the documents before the first interview; a story that only "feels wrong"
    does not survive proceedings. (3) Because the reply is a test the documents either pass or
    fail: a subject who misstates a fact the documents contradict has confirmed the documents,
    and a subject who declines is reported as declining — fairness doubles as the method's own
    verification layer and as legal armour.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Investigation assembles a case, not a story: a hypothesis about wrongdoing tested against records, witnesses and numbers.</li>
    <li>The spine is public documents — filings any citizen could request — with sources adding explanation and direction.</li>
    <li>Story ideas are constructed from paper gaps and must pass the three-part test: so what, prove what, who could sue.</li>
    <li>The master file, the two-source rule and the recorded exact question are what make the case survive lawyers and attacks.</li>
    <li>The bulletproofing pass is adversarial by design: independent fact-checker, document forensics, recomputed numbers, written response file.</li>
    <li>Right-of-reply is evidence, not courtesy — the denial either confirms the documents or is reported as a decline.</li>
    <li>Landmark investigations indict systems, and impact is a duty: follow-ups, owned corrections, sources protected for years.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is the journalism ladder's expert capstone — there is no further rung in the
    subject on this atlas. The two lessons it most rewards revisiting are the advanced pair:
    <em>Data Journalism</em>, whose document-processing pipeline is now a verification
    instrument as much as a production tool, and <em>Media Law and Ethics</em>, where the
    shield-law and source-protection limits this page treated as operational constraints are
    argued in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three frontiers define the expert layer. <strong>The data-native investigation:</strong>
    algorithmic accountability reporting (probing the models that set rents, sentences and
    insurance prices) reverses the classic direction — the subject is a system, and the
    method borrows from auditing and computer science. <strong>The legal environment:</strong>
    source protection remains uneven worldwide (shield laws in some jurisdictions,
    police raids on newsrooms in others), and SLAPP suits — strategic lawsuits against
    public participation — are now a global censorship tool answered by anti-SLAPP
    statutes and cross-border solidarity publishing. <strong>The epistemology:</strong>
    in a synthetic-media era, provenance (authenticated capture, signed footage, the
    provenance-race question from the generative-models lesson) becomes part of the
    reporter's evidence chain — the verification burden grows as the cost of faking
    falls. Through all of it, the discipline's core habit stays the same: check the
    primary source, name the base rate, show the method — the habit this entire atlas
    exists to teach.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Woodward & Bernstein, All the President\'s Men (1974); the Spotlight Team\'s methodology (Boston Globe, 2002); ICIJ collaboration models; the verification and cryptography lessons. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
